{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1778192112,"nonce":"AYzhOXSQz27f2hKhXQX41Id2KKI=","title":"Expose public single-step API on Graph that handles cycle wires","message":"Currently `Graph._call_single_step(inputs, state, *, key)` is the per-timestep computation, but it does NOT thread cycle wires across calls — only `_call_with_iteration` does (via `lax.scan`). External callers wanting per-step evaluation of a graph with cycles (e.g., for analysis tools that need to drive the model one step at a time and inspect intermediate state) currently have no clean public API.\n\n**Concrete failure mode:** `graph._call_single_step(inputs={\"input\": obs}, state=state, key=key)` on a `SimpleFeedback` graph raises `KeyError: 'force'` because the cycle wire `Wire(\"mechanics\", \"state\", \"feedback\", \"mechanics\")` declares an input port (`mechanics`) on the `feedback` node that the caller hasn't provided — it should come from the previous timestep's mechanics state via the cycle, but `_call_single_step` doesn't take previous-step cycle port values as input.\n\n**Surfaced from rlrmp** (`53b5fe5`): the rlrmp induced-gain analyser (`74bfd86`) needs to step a trained `SimpleFeedback` model one timestep at a time as part of computing the closed-loop linearisation. Currently rlrmp works around this with a private `_NetworkController` adapter that wraps `model.nodes['net']` directly and reconstructs the feedback wiring on the rlrmp side — works, but duplicates feedbax-internal logic and breaks the analyser's clean Controller abstraction.\n\n**Proposed API:**\n\n```python\nclass Graph:\n    def step(\n        self,\n        inputs: dict,\n        state: eqx.nn.State,\n        cycle_port_values: dict | None = None,\n        *,\n        key: PRNGKeyArray,\n    ) -\u003e tuple[dict, eqx.nn.State, dict]:\n        \"\"\"Advance the graph by one timestep.\n\n        Args:\n            inputs: External (non-cycle) input port values.\n            state: Current eqx.nn.State (component substates).\n            cycle_port_values: Cycle wire port values from the previous step.\n                Pass None on the first step to use defaults; thereafter pass\n                the dict returned by the previous .step() call.\n            key: PRNGKey for any noise in the step.\n\n        Returns:\n            outputs: Output port values produced this step.\n            new_state: Updated eqx.nn.State.\n            cycle_port_values: Cycle wire port values to pass into next step.\n        \"\"\"\n```\n\nThe implementation should mirror what `_call_with_iteration` does internally for a single iteration of its `lax.scan` body — hoist the cycle threading logic from inside the scan into a callable `step` method, and have `_call_with_iteration` delegate to it.\n\n**Tests:**\n- Smoke: 2-component graph with a back-edge wire; chain `step` calls, verify cycle ports propagate correctly across calls.\n- Equivalence: `Graph.__call__(n_steps=N)` should produce identical results to a manual loop of N `step` calls (carry cycle port values).\n- Stateless graphs (no cycles): `step` works without any `cycle_port_values` argument.\n\n**Why `step` not `__call__` with `n_steps=1`:** the iteration machinery has overhead (`lax.scan` setup, return-state-history allocation) that's wasteful when the caller is driving the loop themselves. A direct `step` is cleaner for analysis use.\n\n**Companion fix in rlrmp** (`53b5fe5`): once this lands on develop, the rlrmp `feedbax_graph_controller` adapter rewrites to use it; the runner workaround in `scripts/run_induced_gain_part2_5.py` becomes unnecessary.","files":null}]}